{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "sint-10-installments-and-finance-plans",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Instalments and Simple-Interest Finance Plans",
    "description": "Value equal instalments at a common date and understand why payment timing matters in debt and finance-plan questions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with easy annual instalments whose future-value factors are visible, then introduce monthly schedules using a common final month. Keep the focus on timing rather than formulas: earlier instalments get more months to earn interest, the last gets none. Use balance and finance-charge checks before the capstone equation.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 22, Simple Interest (printed pp. 641–662). Approved scope: Teach equal annual instalments for a debt due at a future date, the accumulated-value method used by the chapter, monthly instalment schedules, down payments, financed balance, finance charge, and the source's month-weight method for recovering an annual simple-interest rate. Emphasise that instalments paid at different times cannot be treated as if they were all outstanding for the full term."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Instalments and Simple-Interest Finance Plans\n\nValue equal instalments at a common date and understand why payment timing matters in debt and finance-plan questions.\n\nWe will build the idea with small numbers first, then move into the exam-style patterns from the chapter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a debt due at a future date, an instalment paid earlier has time to earn simple interest before the due date. Compare all instalments at the same final date.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ₹660 debt due in 3 years at 10% can be discharged by equal payments x at the ends of years 1, 2, 3. At year 3 their values are 1.2x, 1.1x, x. So 3.3x=660 and x=₹200.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
